DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These functions operate on file descriptors for terminal type devices.

     The iissaattttyy() function determines if the file descriptor _f_d refers to a
     valid terminal type device.

     The ttttyynnaammee() function gets the related device name of a file descriptor
     for which iissaattttyy() is true.

     The ttttyynnaammee() function returns the name stored in a static buffer which
     will be overwritten on subsequent calls.  The ttttyynnaammee__rr() function takes
     a buffer and length as arguments to avoid this problem.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The iissaattttyy() function returns 1 if _f_d refers to a terminal type device;
     otherwise, it returns 0 and may set _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the error.  The
     ttttyynnaammee() function returns the null terminated name if the device is
     found and iissaattttyy() is true; otherwise a NULL pointer is returned.  The
     ttttyynnaammee__rr() function returns 0 if successful.  Otherwise an error number
     is returned.

EERRRROORRSS
     These functions may fail if:

     [EBADF]            The _f_d argument is not a valid file descriptor.

     [ENOTTY]           The file associated with _f_d is not a terminal.

     Additionally, ttttyynnaammee__rr() may fail if:

     [ERANGE]           The _b_u_f_s_i_z_e argument is smaller than the length of the
                        string to be returned.

